## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design automated degradation with controlled risk during external dependency pressure and multi-region operation.

## Key Concepts
- Circuit breakers and failover
- Per-region degradation strategies
- Idempotent queues and exactly-once delivery
- Canary and synthetic testing
- Rollback criteria and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for circuit breaker state machine
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(threshold, resetTime) {
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.resetTime = resetTime;
    this.failures = 0;
    this.state = 'CLOSED';
  }
  onFailure() {
    this.failures++;
    if (this.failures > this.threshold) this.state = 'OPEN';
  }
  canRequest() { return this.state !== 'OPEN'; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for multi-tenant services with mixed SLIs?
- How would you validate degradation paths in canaries without impacting customers?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to craft a practical, beginner-friendly incident plan focused on cache-layer issues, cross-region observability, and safe rollback via feature flags. Emphasizes concrete signals and actionable steps beyond definitions.

## Key Concepts

- Cache-then-DB interaction and blast radius
- Observability signals: logs, metrics, traces
- Safe mitigation via feature flags or bypass paths
- Defined rollback and validation windows

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo cache-bypass toggle
let bypassCache = false
function readData(key){
  if(bypassCache){
    return readFromDb(key)
  }
  const v = readFromCache(key)
  if(v) return v
  return readFromDb(key)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this plan in staging before production?
- What data consistency checks would you add during rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to respond to production incidents with a concrete, beginner-friendly plan focusing on observable signals, containment, rollback, and validation under time pressure.

## Key Concepts
- Observability: latency p95, error rate, queue depth, redis/backlog metrics
- Containment: throttle concurrency, pause new work, revert patch safely
- Rollback & Validation: restore previous config, synthetic tests, end-to-end checks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple end-to-end validation stub
async function checkDelivery(queueClient, producer) {
  const msg = {id: 'test', payload: 'ping'};
  await producer.send(msg);
  const ok = await queueClient.awaitDeliveryAck(msg.id, 5000);
  return ok;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate the rollback trigger and rollback-on-fail thresholds?
- Which metrics would you add to distinguish transient spikes from persistent issues?

## Why This Is Asked

Gauges ability to manage per-tenant QoS under bursty traffic and to design practical safeguards that minimize blast radius.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant QoS and quotas
- Backpressure and prioritization
- Isolation and graceful degradation
- Telemetry, SLO-driven rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate; this.burst = burst; this.tokens = burst; this.last = Date.now();
  }
  allow() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const delta = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + delta * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= 1) { this.tokens -= 1; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for a service mesh with mutual TLS and header-based routing?
- How would you test failover and simulate tenant backoffs in a staging environment?
